1
00:00:00,350 --> 00:00:05,387
Open your form trigger, find "set mock data," and paste in one realistic transcript.

2
00:00:05,387 --> 00:00:10,364
This build was tested against a short, ordinary renewal call between Priya and Ravi

3
00:00:10,364 --> 00:00:15,101
Chandra: some scheduling small talk, and two real commitments buried inside it.

4
00:00:15,101 --> 00:00:17,560
What does mock data look like for a form?

5
00:00:17,560 --> 00:00:22,537
The same shape a real submission would have: a value for each of your three fields,

6
00:00:22,537 --> 00:00:24,756
meeting title, attendees, transcript.

7
00:00:24,756 --> 00:00:29,433
Pin that once and you can rewire and retune everything downstream without ever

8
00:00:29,433 --> 00:00:34,470
re-filling the form, which matters here more than it did for email, because you will

9
00:00:34,470 --> 00:00:39,447
be tweaking the extraction prompt several times and pasting a full transcript in by

10
00:00:39,447 --> 00:00:44,184
hand each round is exactly the sort of friction that makes people stop testing.

11
00:00:44,734 --> 00:00:48,039
That football exchange is doing real work in your test.

12
00:00:48,039 --> 00:00:52,726
If your agent extracts it as an action item, you have found a mistake cheaply,

13
00:00:52,726 --> 00:00:55,249
before a real client call ever reached it.

14
00:00:55,249 --> 00:01:00,237
That is the entire point of testing with something realistically messy instead of a

15
00:01:00,237 --> 00:01:03,842
clean, easy sample that was never going to trip anything up.

16
00:01:03,842 --> 00:01:08,829
When you write your own test transcript, plant one more trap beyond the small talk:

17
00:01:08,829 --> 00:01:11,173
a commitment made and then walked back.

18
00:01:11,173 --> 00:01:16,040
Someone says they will send the figures Thursday, then two lines later, actually,

19
00:01:16,040 --> 00:01:18,323
hold off until the contract is signed.

20
00:01:18,323 --> 00:01:23,130
Real calls do this constantly, and it is the single hardest case for extraction,

21
00:01:23,130 --> 00:01:26,195
because the commitment genuinely was said out loud.

22
00:01:26,195 --> 00:01:31,062
An agent that reports it anyway is obeying the letter of your ceiling and missing

23
00:01:31,062 --> 00:01:32,084
the conversation.

24
00:01:32,084 --> 00:01:37,071
Whether yours handles it, and how, is exactly the kind of thing you want to know on

25
00:01:37,071 --> 00:01:41,938
pinned data, for free, rather than discover in the minutes of a real client call.

26
00:01:42,488 --> 00:01:47,729
This is the single most important habit in this entire course, and it shows up first

27
00:01:47,729 --> 00:01:52,846
here because extraction makes it easiest to see: an AI agent's output always looks

28
00:01:52,846 --> 00:01:55,841
equally confident, whether it is right or wrong.

29
00:01:55,841 --> 00:02:00,396
Reading it critically is not optional, and it never stops being your job.

30
00:02:00,396 --> 00:02:05,451
Notice what tidiness does to your own judgement, because that is the mechanism to

31
00:02:05,451 --> 00:02:06,387
defend against.

32
00:02:06,387 --> 00:02:11,503
A cleanly formatted list borrows the authority of every correct list you have ever

33
00:02:11,503 --> 00:02:16,745
read: the brackets are right, the dates are shaped like dates, and your eye wants to

34
00:02:16,745 --> 00:02:20,489
approve it on grounds that have nothing to do with the call.

35
00:02:20,489 --> 00:02:23,047
The defence is mechanical, not willpower.

36
00:02:23,047 --> 00:02:27,852
Put the transcript and the output side by side and tick each line against the

37
00:02:27,852 --> 00:02:29,536
sentence that justifies it.

38
00:02:29,536 --> 00:02:30,847
No sentence, no tick.

39
00:02:30,847 --> 00:02:36,088
Thirty seconds per item, and it is the same side-by-side habit the spot check rubric

40
00:02:36,088 --> 00:02:38,647
in Section 7 turns into a permanent tool.

41
00:02:39,197 --> 00:02:42,153
The order of the first two steps is not decoration.

42
00:02:42,153 --> 00:02:46,848
Count the items yourself before you look at the agent's answer, because whichever

43
00:02:46,848 --> 00:02:51,080
list you read first becomes the anchor the other one gets judged against.

44
00:02:51,080 --> 00:02:55,775
Read the agent first and its answer quietly replaces your memory of the call; you

45
00:02:55,775 --> 00:02:59,659
will find yourself agreeing with items you could not have produced.

46
00:02:59,659 --> 00:03:02,789
Count first, and your list does the anchoring instead.

47
00:03:02,789 --> 00:03:06,962
And the zero-item transcript in step three is not a throwaway: it is the

48
00:03:06,962 --> 00:03:10,962
escape-hatch test, proving the agent can hand back an honest nothing.

49
00:03:10,962 --> 00:03:15,483
An extraction agent that cannot say "nothing here" will eventually manufacture

50
00:03:15,483 --> 00:03:20,236
something, and you want to know which kind you built while the stakes are a pinned

51
00:03:20,236 --> 00:03:20,642
sample.

52
00:03:20,642 --> 00:03:25,395
With that proof in hand, next lecture connects this agent to a real form and ships

53
00:03:25,395 --> 00:03:25,569
it.
